1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
Chinese language models can score well on general benchmarks,

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:12,000
yet still misread idioms, classical words, or Cantonese grammar.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,232
ChAAt exposes these language-specific gaps.

4
00:00:17,432 --> 00:00:23,500
The demo loads six models, fifty hard items, and three repeated runs.

5
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:31,094
No API key is needed, and no model can access tools or the web.

6
00:00:31,294 --> 00:00:38,000
The overview ranks models from 100 percent down to 33 percent.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The capability map shows where performance breaks.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,321
GPT-4o mini reaches 28 percent on idioms and 13 percent on Classical Chinese.

9
00:00:50,521 --> 00:00:57,000
ChAAt also supports item-level inspection.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
GPT-4o mini interprets ceng ji he shi as “a long time ago.”

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,707
The verified meaning is “not long ago”; the UI shows both answers and the explanation.

12
00:01:11,907 --> 00:01:17,000
The benchmark has three tiers.

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Fifty hard items, 41 exam-style items, and six open-ended tasks.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,994
Answer positions are balanced, and every request is evaluated without external search.

15
00:01:30,194 --> 00:01:36,500
Two people scored all 36 open-ended answers.

16
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:44,132
Two raters scored all 36 answers, followed by a blind third rater; three-rater ICC was 0.96.

17
00:01:44,332 --> 00:01:51,000
ChAAt turns scores into an interpretable capability map and concrete failure cases.

18
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:59,069
It supports model selection, linguistic diagnosis, and reproducible evaluation.
